const LX_C12_P1 = [
{title:"Radio interview with a sustainable fashion designer",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Sisa Maldonado, a fashion designer from Otavalo who makes clothes from vintage fabrics.",voices:{H:V_.dan,G:V_.nic},
script:[["H","Good morning and welcome to Design Matters. With me in the studio is Sisa Maldonado, a designer from Otavalo who now runs a small label in Quito and makes clothes entirely from vintage and second-hand fabrics. Sisa, welcome."],["G","Thank you. It's a pleasure to be here."],["H","Where did the idea of working with old fabrics come from?"],["G","People usually assume it started with some big environmental awakening, maybe a documentary about textile waste, and I have watched plenty of those since. But no, it was far more personal. When my grandmother died, my aunts were about to give away a trunk full of her old shawls and ponchos, some of them woven sixty or seventy years ago. I simply couldn't let them go. I'd just finished a design course in Quito, which, by the way, had nothing to do with recycling, and I spent that whole summer turning them into jackets. A trip to a flea market in London came later, and that confirmed it, but the trunk was the real beginning."],["H","Finding the material must be a challenge. Where do your fabrics come from now?"],["G","All over the place. Second-hand shops in Quito, of course, and the Saturday market in Otavalo, where I still know half the stallholders. Some people even send me things by post, which is lovely. But not in a million years would I have predicted where most of it comes from today: hotels. When a hotel renovates, it throws out curtains, tablecloths, sometimes beautiful heavy linen. I have arrangements with about a dozen hotels now, and they account for more than half of what I use."],["H","Vintage is all the rage at the moment. Do you worry that it's just a passing fad?"],["G","Not in the least. Trends come and go, and I wouldn't lose any sleep over people deciding next year that floral prints are a thing of the past. What concerns me far more is something else. As vintage gets more fashionable, big companies are buying up second-hand clothes by the tonne, and prices in local markets have gone up. Small designers like me, and ordinary families who've always relied on those markets, are being priced out. That's what keeps me awake at night, not whether vintage is cool."],["H","Tell us about how you design. Do you start with a sketch?"],["G","Never. I know that's the conventional way, and my teachers were horrified. I start with the fabric itself. I'll leave a piece on my table for days, sometimes weeks, and just look at it, feel it, notice where it's worn or faded. I don't try to hide the flaws; they're often what decide the shape. A stain might become the place for a pocket. Only once I really know the fabric do I pick up a pencil. And because customers mix and match my pieces with what they already own, everything has to be versatile."],["H","And what's next for the label?"],["G","Everyone expects me to say a shop in New York, and I have had offers to sell abroad, but shipping clothes across the world rather defeats the purpose. I'd also love to write a book one day, but that can wait. What I'm actually doing, starting in March, is running free workshops in Otavalo for young women who want to learn to repair and remake clothes. If they can earn a living from it, that's worth more to me than any boutique."],["H","Sisa Maldonado, thank you."]],
q:[["What first led Sisa to work with old fabrics?","She rescued some of her grandmother's textiles.","She saw a documentary about textile waste.","She visited a flea market in London.","Her design course focused on recycling."],["Where does most of Sisa's fabric come from today?","hotels that are being renovated","second-hand shops in Quito","the Saturday market in Otavalo","people who send her items by post"],["What worries Sisa most about the popularity of vintage?","Second-hand clothes have become too expensive for local people.","It may turn out to be a passing fad.","Floral prints may go out of fashion.","Big companies are copying her designs."],["How does Sisa begin designing a new piece?","by spending time studying the fabric","by drawing a sketch","by asking customers what they already own","by hiding the flaws in the material"],["What is Sisa planning to do next?","teach young women to repair and remake clothes","open a shop in New York","start selling her clothes abroad","write a book about vintage fashion"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with an archaeologist",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Andrés Paredes, an archaeologist who worked on the discovery of ancient cities in the Upano Valley in the Ecuadorian Amazon.",voices:{H:V_.lil,G:V_.ada},
script:[["H","Welcome to Past Lives. In January 2024, a study published in the journal Science revealed a network of ancient cities hidden beneath the forest of the Upano Valley in the Ecuadorian Amazon. My guest, Dr Andrés Paredes, was part of the team. Andrés, welcome."],["G","Thanks, it's great to be here."],["H","How was the discovery actually made?"],["G","Well, the funny thing is that the first mounds weren't found by LiDAR at all. Back in the seventies, the archaeologist Pedro Porras had already described earth platforms near the village of Huapula, and local farmers had been ploughing around them for decades. What nobody knew was how far they extended. In 2015, a plane fitted with a LiDAR scanner flew over the valley, firing laser pulses through gaps in the canopy. Once the trees had been digitally removed, we could see more than six thousand platforms, plazas and roads connecting them. Satellite images had shown nothing at all, because the forest is simply too dense."],["H","Many people were astonished that cities could exist in the Amazon. Why was that?"],["G","For a long time it was widely believed that the rainforest was too poor to support large populations. The soil was considered too thin, and it was assumed that Amazonian societies must have been small groups moving from place to place. Indigenous communities had always said otherwise, of course, but their accounts weren't taken seriously. The Upano sites challenge that narrative entirely. These people grew maize, beans and sweet potatoes on drained fields, and the volcanic soil, enriched by eruptions of the Sangay volcano, made the valley surprisingly fertile."],["H","How old are these cities?"],["G","The earliest occupation seems to date from around two and a half thousand years ago, and the sites are thought to have been inhabited for roughly a thousand years. So they're older than any comparable settlements in the Amazon that we know of."],["H","What surprised you most personally?"],["G","Honestly, the size I'd half expected, and the dates didn't shock me either. What I really hadn't anticipated were the roads. Some run perfectly straight for ten or even twenty kilometres, cutting through hills rather than going around them. Building them must have required enormous coordination. They weren't simply paths; they were deliberate, engineered statements. Had we only worked on the ground, we'd never have pieced that together."],["H","Has the discovery changed how local people see their history?"],["G","Very much so. The valley is home to Shuar communities today, and, as far as we can tell, they aren't direct descendants of the platform builders. But several Shuar leaders told us the findings had confirmed what their elders had always said: that the forest was once full of people. What I'd be wary of is tourism arriving too fast. Several of the sites are on private farmland and haven't been protected yet."],["H","So what's next for the project?"],["G","People keep asking when we'll start large-scale excavations, and the answer is: not soon. Digging destroys as much as it reveals, and we don't have the funding anyway. More LiDAR flights would be lovely, and they'll come eventually. But the priority now is getting the sites legally protected before roads and cattle farms damage them. Once something has been bulldozed, it can never be unearthed again."],["H","Dr Andrés Paredes, thank you."]],
q:[["According to Andrés, what was new about the LiDAR survey?","It showed how far the settlements extended.","It found the first earth platforms in the valley.","It confirmed what satellite images had shown.","It led local farmers to discover the mounds."],["Why had experts doubted that cities existed in the Amazon?","They believed the land could not feed large populations.","Indigenous accounts described only small groups.","They thought volcanic eruptions had destroyed the evidence.","The forest was too dense to excavate."],["What surprised Andrés most about the discovery?","the way the roads had been built","the size of the settlements","the age of the cities","the fertility of the volcanic soil"],["What does Andrés say about the Shuar communities?","The findings supported what their elders had always said.","They are direct descendants of the platform builders.","They have opposed tourism in the valley.","They own most of the archaeological sites."],["What is the team's main priority now?","getting legal protection for the sites","starting large-scale excavations","carrying out more LiDAR flights","developing tourism in the valley"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with an astronomer",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Lucía Andrade, an astronomer at the Quito Astronomical Observatory, about the search for life on other planets.",voices:{H:V_.eri,G:V_.emm},
script:[["H","Good evening and welcome to Science Tonight. My guest is Dr Lucía Andrade, an astronomer at the Quito Astronomical Observatory, which was founded in 1873 and is one of the oldest in South America. Lucía, welcome."],["G","Thank you for having me."],["H","What first drew you to astronomy?"],["G","People tend to assume it was a telescope, or the observatory itself, since I grew up just a few streets away from it. Actually, it was a school trip to Chimborazo when I was twelve. We stayed in the refuge overnight, and I'd never seen so many stars. Our teacher mentioned that, because of the shape of the planet, the summit is the point on Earth closest to space, and I remember thinking, well, if I'm this close, I might as well keep going. My parents would have preferred me to study medicine, if I'm honest."],["H","Let's get to the big question. Is there life out there?"],["G","Well, I'm tempted to say yes, but I have to be careful. As far as we know, there are billions of planets in our galaxy alone, and a good number seem to orbit in the zone where liquid water could exist. Statistically, it would be rather odd if Earth were the only one. But odd isn't evidence. If I'm not mistaken, we haven't got a single confirmed sign of life anywhere beyond Earth. So the honest answer is: it's likely, but unproven."],["H","And what kind of life are scientists actually looking for?"],["G","Not little green men sending us messages, I'm afraid. Most researchers expect that, if we find anything, it'll be microbial: minute organisms like bacteria. And we won't see them directly; no telescope could do that. What we're looking for are biosignatures, gases in a planet's atmosphere that are hard to explain without life. A couple of years ago there was great excitement about a gas detected on a planet called K2-18b. We'll never send a probe there in our lifetimes, of course; it's over a hundred light years away. It's been speculated that the signal was a fluke of the data, and it hasn't been confirmed, so most of us are keeping an open mind."],["H","What role does an observatory like yours play? You're hardly competing with the giant telescopes."],["G","No, not remotely. Most of the observations that matter now come from space telescopes like James Webb. Our position on the equator does have one advantage: we can see both the northern and southern skies. We still publish research, but to be honest, our main contribution these days is education. Every year, we welcome school groups from all over the country, and we run open nights twice a week. If one child in those groups goes on to become a scientist, that's worth more than any paper we publish."],["H","Final question. If we did find life, how would people react?"],["G","Rather less dramatically than films suggest, I suspect. If it's microbes, I imagine there'd be a few days of headlines, and then life would carry on as normal. Some people fear it would shake religious beliefs, but surveys suggest most believers wouldn't feel threatened at all. Personally, I think the biggest change would be philosophical. Knowing that life had begun twice, independently, would suggest it's common in the universe, and that might make us feel a little less alone and a little less special at the same time."],["H","Dr Lucía Andrade, thank you."]],
q:[["What first made Lucía interested in astronomy?","a night she spent on a mountain during a school trip","growing up near the observatory","a telescope she was given as a child","her parents' interest in science"],["What is Lucía's view on whether life exists on other planets?","It is probable, but there is no proof yet.","It definitely exists somewhere in our galaxy.","Earth is probably the only inhabited planet.","It has been confirmed on planets with liquid water."],["According to Lucía, how are scientists most likely to detect life?","by analysing the gases in a planet's atmosphere","by observing microbes directly with telescopes","by receiving messages from intelligent beings","by sending probes to distant planets"],["What does Lucía see as the observatory's main role today?","inspiring young people through education","observing both the northern and southern skies","competing with the largest telescopes","publishing research papers"],["How does Lucía think people would react to the discovery of microbial life?","There would be brief interest, and then life would continue as normal.","There would be panic like the kind shown in films.","Most religious believers would feel threatened.","It would be front-page news for years."]]}
];

const LX_C12_P2 = [
{title:"Five people talk about brands and shopping habits",topic:"brand loyalty",who:["a student","a personal trainer","a retired nurse","a sales assistant","a marketing manager","a young mother","a car mechanic","a fashion blogger"],
op:["I stay with brands because changing feels like too much effort.","Shops use clever tricks to make us spend more.","Ethical values now matter more to me than price.","Expensive brands are often no better than cheaper ones.","Loyalty schemes rarely reward customers fairly.","Online reviews have more influence on me than adverts.","Buying second-hand has changed my attitude to shopping.","Brand loyalty is often inherited from our parents."],
sp:[["People are always amazed when I tell them this, but a lot of the spare parts I fit come off the very same production line, whether they end up in a box with a famous logo or a plain one. A customer last week insisted on the official filter for his car and paid nearly three times what the generic one would have cost. Same factory, same specification, different sticker. I've been fixing cars in Latacunga for twenty years, and honestly, what you're paying for with the big names is mostly the logo and the advertising budget, not the quality.",6,3,"lew"],
["When I set up home with my partner, I did my first big supermarket shop and only realised at the checkout that my trolley was practically a copy of my mum's. The same toothpaste, the same washing powder, even the same tomato sauce she's bought since the nineties. I'd never consciously chosen any of them. My partner's trolley would have looked completely different, because his family always bought other things. It made me wonder how many of our so-called choices are simply habits we picked up in our parents' kitchens before we could even read.",5,7,"hea"],
["I used to be obsessed with new trainers. Every time a limited edition came out, I'd queue for hours. Then, during my first year at university, money got tight and a friend dragged me to a thrift shop in La Floresta. I found a denim jacket from the eighties for five dollars, and it was better made than anything in my wardrobe. Now I hunt for pre-loved clothes almost every weekend, and it's completely transformed the way I think about buying things. I ask myself whether I actually need something, and I enjoy the hunt far more than I ever enjoyed the queue.",0,6,"nic"],
["From the customer's side of the counter, I never noticed any of it. Now I work in a department store, and I can't stop seeing it. The music slows down in the afternoon so that people wander for longer. The essentials are always right at the back, so you have to walk past everything else to reach them. There's a vanilla scent pumped into the homeware section because apparently it makes people feel relaxed. And the little impulse items next to the tills are chosen very carefully. None of it is accidental; every detail is designed to loosen your grip on your wallet.",3,1,"ali"],
["You'd think that someone who spends her days designing campaigns would be easily won over by them. Quite the opposite. I know exactly how much a glossy television commercial costs and how carefully every word is chosen, so I take it all with a pinch of salt. Before I buy anything, a blender, a hotel room, anything at all, I spend ages reading what other customers have written, especially the two-star comments. If twenty strangers complain that the lid leaks, no clever slogan in the world is going to change my mind.",4,5,"sar"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about games and play",topic:"games and play",who:["a primary school teacher","a grandmother","a software engineer","a professional chess player","a child psychologist","an office manager","a toy shop owner","a university lecturer"],
op:["Adults need play just as much as children do.","Losing teaches children valuable lessons.","Screens have not replaced traditional games.","Games can build relationships between generations.","Unstructured play is more valuable than organised activities.","Competition can spoil the fun of a game.","Playing games has helped me at work.","Children today have too little free time to play."],
sp:[["My grandson is eleven, and for a while we had almost nothing to say to each other. He'd sit with his headphones on, and I'd sit with my knitting. Then one rainy afternoon I taught him cuarenta, the card game my father taught me in Ambato. He was hooked. Now, every Sunday, we play for hours, and in exchange he's teaching me one of his video games, which I'm utterly hopeless at, despite his patient explanations. We laugh at each other constantly. Those noisy, silly afternoons have given us a bond I honestly thought we'd lost.",1,3,"lil"],
["Parents often proudly tell me about their child's swimming lessons, robotics club and violin practice. Those things are fine, but in my experience, the real developmental magic happens when nobody is in charge. Give three children a cardboard box and an empty afternoon, and they'll invent a spaceship, argue about the rules, negotiate, compromise and change the story ten times. No coach or carefully planned timetable, however well meant, can teach that. When an adult designs the activity, children learn to follow instructions. When they design it themselves, they learn to think.",4,4,"ada"],
["People assume I must have been a child prodigy who won everything. In fact, until I was about nine, I was beaten in almost every game I played against my uncle, and he never once let me win out of kindness. I used to storm off in tears. But each defeat forced me to work out what I'd done wrong, and slowly I learnt to cope with disappointment without falling apart. I see parents now who let their kids win every single time to protect their feelings, and I think they're depriving them of something important.",3,1,"isa"],
["Somewhere along the way, most of us decide that grown-ups should be serious and that games are for kids. I certainly did. For years my evenings were just dinner, television and bed. Then a neighbour invited me to a weekly board game night at a café near my flat, and I went reluctantly. It's now the highlight of my week. I laugh more, I sleep better and I feel lighter. I'm convinced that losing ourselves in something silly and pointless isn't childish at all. It's something every one of us requires, whatever our age.",5,0,"dan"],
["I grew up playing puzzle games, the colourful kind where you have to rotate pipes or move blocks to open a door, and my parents thought I was wasting my time. Funnily enough, those games are probably the reason I'm good at what I do now. When I'm hunting for a bug in a huge program, it's exactly the same feeling: test an idea, fail, try another approach, stay patient. My boss once asked how I solve problems so quickly. The honest answer is years of practice on a games console, although I didn't dare tell him that.",2,6,"eri"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about spending time in nature",topic:"nature and well-being",who:["a city lawyer","a park ranger","a yoga teacher","a hospital doctor","a retired miner","a teenage student","a landscape photographer","a café owner"],
op:["Nature helps me deal with stress better than anything else.","You don't need to travel far to benefit from nature.","Some claims about forest bathing are exaggerated.","Being in nature makes me more creative.","Visitors often damage the places they love.","Silence in nature can be uncomfortable at first.","Children should spend more time outdoors at school.","Nature has helped me recover from illness."],
sp:[["I'm all for people getting out among the trees; I recommend it to my patients regularly. Fresh air and gentle walking do lower blood pressure, and there's decent research on mood. But I get frustrated by some of the websites I read. They say breathing in tree oils will cure everything from insomnia to cancer, and that one weekend in the forest strengthens your immune system for a month. The evidence for that simply isn't there yet. The real benefits are lovely and well worth having; there's no need to inflate them.",3,2,"mic"],
["I've worked in Cotopaxi National Park for fifteen years, and I've never met a visitor who didn't say how much they adored it. The trouble is, that affection often comes at a cost for the landscape. People leave the paths to get the perfect photo and trample plants that take decades to grow back at this altitude. They feed the foxes, which then stop hunting. Every weekend we collect bags of plastic bottles and snack wrappers from the lagoon. Most of them would be horrified to think they were harming the páramo, but collectively, they are.",1,4,"fab"],
["My sister booked me a forest bathing session in Mindo as a birthday present, and I'll admit the first hour was awful. The guide asked us to switch off our phones and walk without talking, and all I could hear was birds, water and my own thoughts. I'm used to constant noise: calls, traffic, meetings. The quiet felt almost threatening, and I kept wanting to check the time. Somewhere in the second hour, though, something shifted and I stopped fighting it. By the end, I didn't want to leave, and I drove back to Quito feeling strangely light.",0,5,"bel"],
["After thirty years underground, my lungs weren't in good shape, and when I had a heart operation two years ago, the doctors weren't sure I'd ever walk any distance again. My daughter started taking me to the hills above our town in Zaruma, just ten minutes at first, then half an hour. The clean air, the green, the effort of the slopes, it all built me back up. Last month I walked for three hours without stopping. The cardiologist says the medication played its part, of course, but I know what really got me back on my feet.",4,7,"lew"],
["Everyone at school talks about trips to the Galápagos or the Amazon as if that's the only real nature. My family can't afford anything like that. But there's a tiny park two streets from our flat in Guayaquil, with a few big trees and a pond, and I go there almost every evening to sit and read. Watching the iguanas and the birds for twenty minutes does more for my mood than any holiday photo I've seen online. Honestly, the benefits are right there on my doorstep, and they don't cost a cent.",5,1,"sar"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about how history is taught and remembered",topic:"history",who:["a secondary school history teacher","a museum guide","a politician","a novelist","a university student","a grandfather","a film director","a tour guide in Quito"],
op:["History lessons focus too much on dates and names.","Ordinary people's stories are often left out.","Monuments should be explained rather than removed.","Films can make people curious about the past.","Every generation rewrites history to suit itself.","Older relatives are a valuable source of history.","Visiting historical sites brings the past to life.","History should be taught without taking sides."],
sp:[["At my school in Ibarra, history meant memorising endless lists: the year of the Battle of Pichincha, every president in order, the year each constitution was signed. I could recite all of it for the exam and forget it a week later. Nobody ever asked us why things happened or how ordinary people felt about them at the time. It's only at university that I discovered history is really about arguments and evidence. Looking back, I wish I'd been taught to question sources at fifteen instead of just learning facts off by heart.",4,0,"lil"],
["In our museum there's a statue of a colonial governor that some visitors find offensive, and I understand why. A few people have demanded that we put it in storage. Personally, I think hiding it away in a basement would let him off the hook far too easily. Instead, we've placed a panel beside it that describes, quite bluntly, how the Indigenous people in his region were treated. Visitors now stop, read and talk about it, sometimes for twenty minutes or more. A statue on its own tells a one-sided story; a statue with context starts a conversation.",1,2,"dan"],
["My last novel is set in Esmeraldas in the 1950s. I did read a great deal, but the best material came from my great-aunt, who's ninety-two. I spent months sitting with her, recording her memories: what they ate, which songs were on the radio, how the floods came. She remembered details no archive could ever give me, right down to the smell of the market. Too often we ignore the elderly people in our own families, and when they go, all of that knowledge disappears with them. So my advice is simple: talk to your grandparents while you still can.",3,5,"emm"],
["When I was researching my documentary about the Liberal Revolution of 1895, I read school textbooks from different decades, and it was fascinating. In the 1930s, the same events were described in a completely different light from the 1970s, and differently again in the books my nephews use today. Each version reflected the worries of its own time: who was in power, what the country wanted to believe about itself. I don't think anyone was deliberately lying to pupils. It's just that every era looks back and finds the story it needs.",6,4,"ada"],
["I take groups around the old town every day, and I've seen it happen hundreds of times. A teenager who's been utterly bored by school textbooks all his life stands in the Plaza Grande, I tell him that the first cry of independence took place just a few streets away, and suddenly he's full of questions. When you can touch the stone walls and walk the same narrow streets, the past stops being words on a page. It becomes real, and personal. No classroom, however well equipped, can compete with that.",7,6,"hea"]]}
];

const CL_C12_1 = [
{title:"Set 1",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a fashion trend.",q:"What is her attitude to the trend?",o:["She likes it but finds it impractical.","She thinks it will soon disappear.","She is surprised that it has become popular."],a:0,lines:[["emm","My teenage daughter keeps telling me that chunky trainers are all the rage, and that I'm hopelessly out of touch. To be fair, I wore almost identical ones in the nineties, so it hardly comes as a shock that they're back. What I can't get my head around is how anyone walks in them on the cobbled streets of Quito's old town. I tried a pair on last weekend, and they felt wonderful on the shop's carpet, but I nearly twisted my ankle on the way home. They're fun, just not for everyday life."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a board game they played.",q:"What do they disagree about?",o:["how long the game lasted","whether Pablo deserved to win","whether the rules were too complicated"],a:2,lines:[["nic","That new strategy game was brilliant, wasn't it? I loved building the railways."],["lew","It was good fun, but honestly, the rule book was a nightmare. We spent the first forty minutes just working out what the counters were for."],["nic","Really? I thought the rules were quite logical once you got going. And it's not as if it took all night."],["lew","No, fair enough, we'd finished by ten. And Pablo thoroughly deserved to win, I'll give him that."],["nic","Absolutely. He was miles ahead."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a visit to a museum.",q:"What impressed him most?",o:["the interactive displays","the knowledge of the guide","the age of the objects"],a:1,lines:[["dan","We went to the Pumapungo Museum in Cuenca on Saturday. The archaeological pieces are extraordinary, some of them well over a thousand years old, though I must say the interactive screens were a bit of a letdown; half of them weren't working. But the woman who showed us round was something else. She could answer every question the kids threw at her, and she seemed to know the story behind every single pot and mask. I could have listened to her all afternoon."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about advertising.",q:"What is his main point?",o:["Advertisers target children unfairly.","Adverts work even when we think we ignore them.","Online adverts are more effective than television ones."],a:1,lines:[["mic","Most people tell me they're immune to advertising. They skip the ads on videos, they scroll past sponsored posts, and they're convinced none of it gets through. Yet when I ask them to name a fizzy drink or a mobile network, the same three names come up every time. That's the point. Repetition goes under the radar. You don't need to pay attention to an advert for it to shape what feels familiar, and familiar feels safe when you're standing in a supermarket aisle. Whether it's on television or on your phone makes very little difference."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a news report about an asteroid.",q:"What does the report say about the asteroid?",o:["It poses no danger to Earth.","Scientists are still calculating its path.","It will be visible without a telescope."],a:0,lines:[["ali","Astronomers have confirmed that the asteroid discovered last month will pass Earth safely in 2032. Early estimates had suggested a small chance of impact, which, unsurprisingly, attracted a great deal of attention online. However, new observations have allowed scientists to calculate its orbit precisely, and the risk has now been ruled out completely. Unfortunately for amateur stargazers, the object is far too small and dark to be seen with the naked eye, even at its closest point."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about walking in the countryside.",q:"Why did she start going for walks?",o:["Her doctor recommended it.","She wanted to lose weight.","A neighbour persuaded her."],a:2,lines:[["sar","People assume I took up walking because of my health, and it's true that my doctor had been nagging me to exercise more for years. I never listened, of course. And I wasn't remotely interested in losing weight. What actually happened was that my neighbour, Rosa, simply refused to take no for an answer. She turned up at my door every Saturday at seven with two flasks of coffee until I gave in. Now it's me who drags her out to the páramo."]]}]},
{title:"Set 2",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about vintage clothes.",q:"Why does he prefer vintage clothes?",o:["They are cheaper than new clothes.","They are better made.","They make him look unique."],a:1,lines:[["fab","I'd love to say I buy vintage to save the planet or because I want to look different from everyone else, but the truth is less noble. A lot of people assume second-hand means cheap, and actually, good vintage pieces in Quito can cost more than new ones. What sells it for me is the stitching. A wool coat from the seventies was made to last thirty years; most things on the high street fall apart after a couple of washes. I'd rather pay a bit more for something that's built properly."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about family game nights.",q:"How does she feel about her son's reaction to losing?",o:["embarrassed","amused","concerned"],a:2,lines:[["bel","We've started a family game night on Fridays, and on the whole it's been lovely. My daughter adores anything with dice. My son, though, is another story. Whenever he lands on the wrong square or someone else wins, he sweeps all the counters off the board. My husband finds it hilarious and says he'll grow out of it, but I'm not so sure. He's nine now, not four, and it's beginning to affect his friendships at school too. I think we need to have a word with his teacher."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a new monument.",q:"What does the woman think about the monument?",o:["It is in the wrong place.","It cost too much money.","It does not represent the person well."],a:0,lines:[["hea","Have you seen the new monument to Dolores Cacuango in the park?"],["eri","I have. It's about time she had one, to be honest. Though I heard it cost a fortune."],["hea","Well, most of the money was raised through donations, so that doesn't bother me. And the sculptor's done a brilliant job; it really captures her strength. My only problem is where they've put it. It's hidden behind the car park, where nobody ever walks."],["eri","Hmm, I hadn't thought of that."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about an advertisement.",q:"Why did he complain about it?",o:["It contained misleading information.","It was shown too often.","It was offensive to older people."],a:0,lines:[["lew","I don't normally get worked up about adverts. They come on far too often during football matches, but I just go and make a cup of tea. This one, though, was different. It was for a mobile phone plan, and it claimed you'd get unlimited data for ten dollars a month. When I signed up, I found out the speed drops to almost nothing after five gigabytes. That isn't unlimited in any sense I understand. My father thought the old man in the advert was a bit insulting, but that didn't bother me. So I sent a formal complaint, and I'm still waiting for a reply."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a news story on the radio.",q:"What is her main point?",o:["The discovery has been exaggerated in the media.","The rock sample is already on its way to Earth.","Chemical explanations have now been ruled out."],a:0,lines:[["isa","So, the headlines this morning are saying that NASA has found life on Mars, which, I'm afraid, isn't quite what the scientists said. What the rover has found are patterns in a rock that, on Earth, are usually produced by microbes. That's exciting, and it's arguably the most promising clue we've had so far. But there are chemical processes that could produce the same patterns without any life at all. Until a sample is brought back to Earth and examined properly, we simply can't say."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a forest bathing session.",q:"What did he find most difficult?",o:["walking slowly","leaving his phone behind","sitting in silence"],a:2,lines:[["ada","I'd read that forest bathing was all about going slowly, and I expected that to drive me mad, because I normally walk everywhere at top speed. In fact, I quite enjoyed it. The guide asked us to leave our phones in a box at the entrance, and I handed mine over without a second thought. What I really struggled with was the twenty minutes we spent sitting alone by the river without saying a word. My mind just wouldn't stop racing."]]}]},
{title:"Set 3",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about what to wear to a wedding.",q:"What does the man decide to do?",o:["rent a suit","buy a new suit","borrow his brother's jacket"],a:0,lines:[["dan","I've got nothing to wear to Carla's wedding. I was thinking of buying a new suit."],["bel","What, for one day? You've got that navy jacket. Throw it on with a white shirt and you're done."],["dan","It doesn't fit me anymore. I could borrow my brother's, I suppose, but he's much taller than me."],["bel","Then what about renting one? There's a place near the shopping centre that does it."],["dan","Oh, that's a much better idea. It'll be cheaper than buying, and they'll alter it for me."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a board game he designed.",q:"What was the main problem when he tested it?",o:["The rules were unclear.","The game took too long.","Starting first gave players an unfair advantage."],a:2,lines:[["mic","I spent a whole year designing a board game about the volcanoes of Ecuador, and I was quite proud of it. When I finally tested it with friends, they understood the rules almost immediately, which was a relief. A game lasted about an hour, which is just right. The trouble was that whoever went first had a huge head start. In ten games, the first player won nine times. So it's back to the drawing board. I need to find a way to balance it."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a museum curator talking about returning objects to their countries of origin.",q:"What is her opinion?",o:["All objects should be returned.","Each case should be judged separately.","Returning objects would harm museums."],a:1,lines:[["emm","I work in a museum in London, so people expect me to defend keeping everything exactly where it is. I can't do that with a clear conscience. Some objects were clearly taken by force, and those should go home, full stop. But it isn't always that simple. Some were bought legally or given as gifts, and a few would genuinely be at risk if they were moved. So I'd say each object has to be looked at on its own merits, rather than following one rule for everything."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about an online post.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The influencer should have said it was an advert.","The product is good value.","Influencers earn too much money."],a:0,lines:[["nic","Did you see Valentina's post about that skin cream? She says it changed her life."],["fab","Yes, and I nearly bought it. Then I found out the company's paying her. She didn't mention that anywhere."],["nic","Exactly. That's what bothers me. I don't mind people getting paid, it's her job, but she should be upfront about it."],["fab","Absolutely. Mind you, I still think forty dollars for a tiny pot is a bit steep."],["nic","Oh, I don't know. Good creams aren't cheap."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about watching a meteor shower.",q:"What does he say about the experience?",o:["He saw fewer meteors than expected.","The weather spoiled the view.","He arrived too late to see much."],a:0,lines:[["eri","We drove up to the Chimborazo refuge to watch the Perseids, because everyone says the sky's clearer up there. And it was: not a single cloud, which in August is something of a miracle. We got there in plenty of time and had our chairs set up by nine. But the forecasts had talked about a hundred meteors an hour, and we counted maybe fifteen. Apparently the moon was too bright. Still, the views of the Milky Way were absolutely stunning, so it wasn't a wasted trip."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about volunteering on a mangrove project.",q:"What surprised her about the project?",o:["how quickly the mangroves grew","how many local people took part","how hard the physical work was"],a:1,lines:[["lil","I joined a mangrove restoration project near Esmeraldas last summer. I knew the work would be tough, planting seedlings knee-deep in mud in the heat, and it was, but I'd been warned. And the seedlings grow slowly, just as everyone had told me. What I hadn't expected was the number of fishing families who turned up every morning to help. They understood better than anyone that the mangroves shield the coastline and protect the fish they depend on."]]}]}
];

const CL_C12_2 = [
{title:"A guided tour of Ingapirca",intro:"You will hear a guide called Marcela Guamán introducing a tour of Ingapirca, the largest Inca site in Ecuador.",voice:"emm",
text:"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Ingapirca. My name is Marcela Guamán, and I'll be your guide this morning. Before we start walking, I'd like to give you a little background, because what you're about to see is the work not of one culture, but of two.\nThe name Ingapirca means 'wall of the Inca' in Kichwa, and it's the largest known Inca site in Ecuador. But long before the Incas arrived, this hill was a sacred place for the Cañari people, who had lived in this region for centuries. When the Inca army came north in the late fifteenth century, the Cañari resisted fiercely. In the end, rather than destroying the site, the Incas built on top of it, and the two peoples ended up sharing it.\nOur first stop will be Pilaloma, the oldest part of the complex. Archaeologists excavating here in the 1970s unearthed a tomb containing the remains of a woman, surrounded by around ten other bodies and many offerings. It is believed that she was a high priestess, although, as with much here, that is still debated.\nFrom there, we'll climb to the most famous structure, the Temple of the Sun. You'll notice it has an unusual elliptical shape. Look closely at the stones. They were cut so precisely that no mortar was needed to hold them together, and they have survived numerous earthquakes. The temple is thought to have been used as an observatory as well as a place of worship: on the solstices, the sunlight falls in line with its doorways.\nMany visitors ask me how the stones were moved. Honestly, we don't know for certain. There were no wheels and no horses, so it must have been done with ropes, ramps and an enormous amount of human labour.\nAfter the temple, we'll walk along a short section of the Qhapaq Ñan, the great Inca road that once connected Quito with Cusco. It's now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and it's quite moving to think of the messengers who ran along it, passing information from one runner to the next.\nIf you have any energy left, there's a twenty-minute trail down to the Cara del Inca, a natural rock face that looks remarkably like a human profile. Children usually love it.\nA few practical points. We're at over three thousand metres here, so please walk slowly and drink plenty of water. The sun is much stronger than it feels, so do put on a hat. And please don't climb on the walls; every year, a little more damage is done by visitors who simply want a better photo.\nFinally, at the end of the tour, I strongly recommend the small site museum near the entrance. It displays Cañari pottery and tools that help piece together the story you'll have heard this morning.\nRight, if everyone's ready, let's begin.",
items:[["Before the Incas arrived, the hill was a sacred place for the ___ people.",["Cañari","Canari"]],["The oldest part of the complex is called ___.",["Pilaloma"]],["The woman found in the tomb is believed to have been a ___.",["high priestess","priestess"]],["The Temple of the Sun has an unusual ___ shape.",["elliptical"]],["The stones were cut so precisely that no ___ was needed.",["mortar"]],["The temple may also have been used as an ___.",["observatory"]],["The stones were probably moved with ropes, ramps and a lot of ___.",["human labour","labour","human labor","labor"]],["The Qhapaq Ñan once connected Quito with ___.",["Cusco","Cuzco"]],["The Cara del Inca is a rock face that looks like a human ___.",["profile"]],["The site museum displays Cañari ___ and tools.",["pottery"]]]},
{title:"The Galápagos and biodiversity hotspots",intro:"You will hear a marine biologist called Tomás Herrera giving a talk about the Galápagos and biodiversity hotspots.",voice:"lew",
text:"Good afternoon. I'm Tomás Herrera, and I've spent the last twelve years studying marine life in the Galápagos. Today I want to talk about why these islands matter so much, and what they can teach us about biodiversity hotspots around the world.\nFirst, a definition. A biodiversity hotspot is a region that is extraordinarily rich in species found nowhere else, and that has already lost at least seventy per cent of its original natural habitat. There are only around thirty-six of them on the planet, and together they cover a tiny fraction of the Earth's land surface. Yet they're home to more than half of all plant species. Ecuador, remarkably, sits inside two of them: the tropical Andes and the Chocó region on the coast.\nStrictly speaking, the Galápagos aren't on that official list, but they are a textbook example of what makes such places special. Because the islands are so isolated, almost a thousand kilometres from the mainland, the species that reached them evolved in their own way. Around eighty per cent of the land birds and nearly all of the reptiles are endemic, which means they exist nowhere else on Earth.\nWhy should we care? Biodiversity isn't just about beautiful animals. Healthy ecosystems work for us. Forests absorb carbon dioxide. Mangroves shield coastlines from storms. Fungi and insects enrich the soil that our food grows in. When species disappear, these services begin to fail, often in ways we only notice when it's too late.\nThe biggest threat to the Galápagos today isn't climate change, although that's serious, but invasive species. Goats, rats and blackberry plants were brought by humans, and they spread like wildfire. On Santiago Island, goats once stripped entire hillsides bare.\nThere is good news, though. Over roughly a decade, an eradication programme removed well over a hundred thousand goats from several islands, and the vegetation has recovered surprisingly quickly. Then there are the giant tortoises. On Española Island, the population had fallen to just fifteen animals. Through a captive breeding programme, scientists managed to restore the population to more than two thousand. One male, Diego, is said to have fathered hundreds of young on his own.\nHuman impact, however, is still growing. The number of visitors has risen sharply, and every boat, every plane and every tourist's shoes can carry seeds or insects. That's why visitors' luggage is checked so carefully on arrival.\nSo what can you do? You don't need to be a scientist. Choose operators that follow park rules, avoid single-use plastics, and if you have a garden, avoid harmful fertilizers that end up in rivers and the sea. Small actions, multiplied by millions of people, really do make a difference.\nThank you.",
items:[["A hotspot must have lost at least ___ of its original natural habitat.",["seventy per cent","70 per cent","70%","seventy percent","70 percent"]],["Hotspots are home to more than half of all ___.",["plant species"]],["Ecuador sits inside ___ biodiversity hotspots.",["two","2"]],["The Galápagos are almost a thousand kilometres from the ___.",["mainland"]],["Species that exist nowhere else are described as ___.",["endemic"]],["Mangroves shield ___ from storms.",["coastlines"]],["According to the speaker, the biggest threat to the Galápagos today is ___.",["invasive species"]],["On Santiago Island, goats once stripped entire ___ bare.",["hillsides"]],["On Española Island, the tortoise population had fallen to just ___ animals.",["fifteen","15"]],["Visitors' ___ is checked carefully on arrival.",["luggage"]]]},
{title:"The psychology of persuasive advertising",intro:"You will hear a former advertising executive called Carolina Mejía giving a talk about persuasive advertising techniques.",voice:"sar",
text:"Hello, everyone. I'm Carolina Mejía, and for fifteen years I worked in advertising agencies in Bogotá and Quito before I started teaching consumer psychology. Today I want to show you some of the techniques advertisers use to sway your opinions, often without you noticing.\nLet's start with scarcity. Phrases like 'only three left' or 'offer ends at midnight' create a sense of urgency. When we think something is about to run out, we value it more and decide faster, which means we think less. Online shops are experts at this; some of those countdown timers simply start again when you refresh the page. Airlines do something similar when they tell you that only two seats are left at this price.\nThe second technique is social proof. We're social animals, and we tend to copy what others do. That's why adverts tell you that nine out of ten customers recommend a product, or show you how many people are looking at the same hotel room right now. Reviews and star ratings tap into exactly the same instinct.\nThird, there's anchoring. If the first price you see is very high, everything after it looks reasonable. Restaurants sometimes put one outrageously expensive dish at the top of the menu, not because they expect to sell it, but so that the second most expensive dish feels like a bargain.\nThen there's the decoy effect. Imagine a cinema selling small popcorn for three dollars and large popcorn for seven. Most people buy the small one. Now add a medium for six dollars fifty. Suddenly the large looks like great value, and sales of the large popcorn go up dramatically. The medium was never really meant to be bought.\nAdvertisers also rely heavily on emotion rather than information. Think of the last advert that really moved you. Chances are it told a story about family, belonging or nostalgia, and the product only appeared at the very end. Research suggests that emotional campaigns are more effective than rational ones, especially in the long term, because we remember feelings far longer than we remember facts or figures.\nLanguage matters too. Alliteration and rhythm make slogans memorable, and words like 'free', 'new' and 'natural' have an almost magical effect, even when they mean very little. 'Natural', in particular, isn't legally defined for most products.\nFinally, personalisation. Every click you make is recorded, and algorithms use that data to show you adverts at the moment you're most susceptible: late at night, for example, when your willpower is lower.\nNone of this is necessarily illegal, and not all of it is harmful. But knowing the tricks gives you a little more control. My advice is simple: when you feel rushed, slow down. If an offer is genuinely good, it'll still be good tomorrow.\nThank you very much.",
items:[["Carolina now teaches ___.",["consumer psychology"]],["Phrases like 'only three left' create a sense of ___.",["urgency"]],["Some countdown timers start again when you ___ the page.",["refresh"]],["Reviews and star ratings are examples of ___.",["social proof"]],["An expensive dish at the top of a menu makes the second most expensive one feel like a ___.",["bargain"]],["In the cinema example, sales of the ___ popcorn go up dramatically.",["large"]],["Emotional adverts often tell a story about family, belonging or ___.",["nostalgia"]],["Alliteration and ___ make slogans memorable.",["rhythm"]],["The word 'natural' isn't ___ defined for most products.",["legally"]],["Algorithms may show adverts late at night, when your ___ is lower.",["willpower"]]]},
{title:"The Royal Game of Ur and the history of board games",intro:"You will hear a historian called Richard Cole giving a talk about the history of board games.",voice:"dan",
text:"Good evening, and thank you for coming to this month's talk at the museum. My name is Richard Cole, and I'm a historian of games. Tonight I'd like to take you back almost five thousand years, to a time when people were already gathering around a board, rolling dice and arguing about whose turn it was.\nThe oldest board game we have clear evidence for is probably senet, from ancient Egypt. Boards have been found in tombs dating back over five thousand years, and the young pharaoh Tutankhamun was buried with several of them. Many historians believe senet was more than entertainment: it may have represented the soul's journey to the afterlife.\nBut the game I want to focus on tonight is the Royal Game of Ur. In the 1920s, the British archaeologist Leonard Woolley was excavating the Royal Cemetery of Ur, in what is now southern Iraq, when he unearthed several beautifully decorated boards. They're about four and a half thousand years old, and the finest one is now in the British Museum.\nFor decades, nobody knew how to play it. Then, in the 1980s, a curator called Irving Finkel came across something remarkable: a clay tablet written in cuneiform by a Babylonian scribe around 177 BC, which described the rules. It's quite something to think that those instructions survived on clay for over two thousand years.\nSo how does it work? It's a race game for two players. Each player has seven counters and has to move them all along a route of twenty squares and off the board before their opponent does. Instead of the cube-shaped dice we use today, players threw four small pyramid-shaped dice. Certain squares are decorated with a rosette, and if you land on one, you get another throw. You can also land on an opponent's counter and send it back to the start, which, as you can imagine, led to some heated moments.\nWhat I find most fascinating is how far the game travelled. It spread across the Middle East and beyond, and, amazingly, a version was still being played by the Jewish community of Kochi, in southern India, until the twentieth century.\nBoard games have always reflected the societies that played them. Chess, which developed in India around the sixth century, mirrored the structure of an army. And Monopoly, which many people assume celebrates getting rich, was actually based on a game designed by a woman called Lizzie Magie in 1904 to criticise greedy landlords. The irony is hard to miss.\nWhy have games lasted so long? I think it's because they offer something we never stop needing: a safe space to compete, to take risks and to lose without real consequences, while sitting face to face with other people.\nAfter the talk, you're welcome to try a replica of the Ur board at the back of the room. Thank you.",
items:[["Senet may have represented the soul's journey to the ___.",["afterlife"]],["The young pharaoh ___ was buried with several senet boards.",["Tutankhamun"]],["The Royal Cemetery of Ur is in what is now ___.",["southern Iraq","Iraq"]],["The finest board from Ur is now in the ___.",["British Museum"]],["The rules were found on a clay tablet written in ___.",["cuneiform"]],["In the Royal Game of Ur, each player has ___ counters.",["seven","7"]],["The counters move along a route of ___ squares.",["twenty","20"]],["If you land on a square with a ___, you get another throw.",["rosette"]],["A version of the game was played in Kochi until the ___.",["twentieth century","20th century"]],["Monopoly was based on a game designed to criticise greedy ___.",["landlords"]]]}
];

const CL_C12_4 = [
{title:"Interview with a forest bathing guide",intro:"You will hear an interview with Daniela Vinueza, a guide who leads forest bathing walks in the cloud forest around Mindo.",voices:{I:V_.dan,G:V_.nic},
script:[["I","My guest today is Daniela Vinueza, a certified forest therapy guide who leads forest bathing walks in the cloud forest around Mindo, about two hours from Quito. Daniela, welcome."],["G","Thanks so much. It's lovely to be here."],["I","Let's start with the obvious question. What exactly is forest bathing? I imagine some listeners are picturing people in swimming costumes."],["G","They wouldn't be the first! It's a translation of the Japanese term shinrin-yoku, which was introduced in the 1980s, partly as a response to stress among office workers. The bathing is metaphorical: you immerse yourself in the atmosphere of the forest using all your senses. It isn't hiking. On a typical walk, we might cover less than a kilometre in three hours. The point isn't to get anywhere."],["I","How did you get into it?"],["G","Rather by accident. I was working as a marketing manager in Quito, and I'd been feeling burnt out for months: not sleeping, constant headaches. My doctor suggested medication, which I didn't want, and a friend suggested yoga, which I tried and, frankly, hated. Then I came to Mindo for a birdwatching weekend, and a local guide took our group on what he called a slow walk. I'd never paid so much attention to anything in my life. Within a year, I'd quit my job and trained as a guide."],["I","There are a lot of health claims made about forest bathing: lower stress hormones, a stronger immune system. How much of that do you believe?"],["G","I try to be careful. There's fairly solid evidence that time among trees can lower cortisol, the stress hormone, and reduce blood pressure, at least in the short term. Some Japanese studies suggest that phytoncides, the oils that trees release, may boost certain immune cells, but those studies were quite small. What I won't do is tell anyone it cures serious ailments. I've had clients ask whether they could stop taking their medication, and I always say absolutely not. It's a complement, not a replacement."],["I","What actually happens on one of your walks?"],["G","We begin at the edge of the forest with a few minutes of breathing, just noticing. Then I give invitations; I never call them instructions. For example, I might ask people to find something that's moving, or to touch the bark of three different trees with their eyes closed. Later, we sit alone for about twenty minutes, and that's often the hardest part. And we always finish with a little ceremony where I serve tea made from plants we've found along the way. That's when people tend to open up and share how they're feeling."],["I","Who comes on your walks?"],["G","I expected mainly foreign tourists, and there are some, but the biggest group, by far, is people from Quito in their thirties and forties, often with very demanding jobs. Interestingly, I also get a lot of grandparents bringing their teenage grandchildren. The teenagers arrive glued to their phones, grumbling, and leave surprisingly calm. That's one of my favourite things to see."],["I","Mindo has become very popular. Is tourism a threat to the forest?"],["G","It can be: zip-lines, more cars, more construction. I don't want to sound like a hypocrite, because I bring people here too. But slow tourism is about the gentlest kind there is. We stay on existing paths, we take nothing away, and I keep groups to a maximum of eight. Part of every fee also goes to a local reserve that protects the habitat of the Andean cock-of-the-rock. If visitors understand why the forest matters, they're far more likely to protect it."],["I","Finally, what about people who can't get to a forest?"],["G","Honestly, you don't need the Amazon or a cloud forest. A park, a tree on your street, even a balcony with plants will do. The key is attention. Put your phone away, slow down and notice what's around you. Having done that every day for a week, most people feel the difference. Nature is more generous than we think."]],
q:[["According to Daniela, how is forest bathing different from hiking?","It involves swimming in rivers.","Covering a long distance is not the aim.","It can only be practised in Japan.",1],["What led Daniela to become a forest bathing guide?","Her doctor recommended it to her.","A walk she did during a weekend away.","She discovered it through yoga classes.",1],["What is Daniela's attitude to the health claims about forest bathing?","She thinks some are better supported than others.","She believes it can replace medication.","She thinks none of them have been proven.",0],["According to Daniela, when do participants usually talk about how they feel?","during the breathing exercises","while they sit alone","during the tea ceremony at the end",2],["Who makes up the largest group of Daniela's clients?","foreign tourists","professionals from Quito","teenagers and their grandparents",1],["How does Daniela try to reduce her impact on the forest?","by limiting the size of her groups","by avoiding the most popular areas","by refusing to work with tourists",0],["What is Daniela's final advice?","Visit a cloud forest at least once.","Pay attention to the nature around you, wherever you are.","Spend at least an hour a day outdoors.",1]]},
{title:"Interview with a designer of educational board games",intro:"You will hear an interview with Martín Cueva, a former teacher who now designs educational board games.",voices:{I:V_.isa,G:V_.eri},
script:[["I","My guest today is Martín Cueva, a former secondary school teacher from Cuenca who now designs educational board games. His game Voltage, in which players build an electricity grid across Ecuador, is now used in more than two hundred schools. Martín, welcome."],["G","Thank you. It's great to be here."],["I","How did you go from teaching to designing games?"],["G","I taught physics for eleven years, and the topic my students found most discouraging was electricity. Their eyes glazed over the moment I started on circuits. I tried videos, experiments, even a field trip to a hydroelectric plant, which they enjoyed, but they'd forgotten everything by the following week. Then one weekend I watched my nephews playing a strategy game for four hours without a break. It was so frustrating to see that level of concentration and know I couldn't get it in class. So I made a rough prototype out of cardboard and tried it with one group. The difference was astonishing."],["I","What makes a good educational game? There are plenty of dull ones out there."],["G","There certainly are! The classic mistake is what I call chocolate-covered broccoli: you take a worksheet, add some dice and call it a game. Kids see through that instantly. In a good educational game, the learning is the mechanism, not a reward stuck on top of it. In Voltage, you can't win unless you balance supply and demand on your grid. Nobody explains the physics to you; you discover it, because otherwise your cities have blackouts."],["I","How long does it take to design a game like that?"],["G","Far longer than people imagine. Voltage took almost three years. The first version took four hours to play, which is no good when a lesson lasts forty-five minutes, so that had to change. I must have tested it with over fifty groups. But the hardest thing wasn't the playing time or the artwork. It was making it fair. In early versions, whoever built a hydroelectric dam first virtually always won, which, given how much Ecuador relies on hydropower, was realistic, but no fun at all."],["I","Do teachers resist using games? Some might think it isn't serious learning."],["G","A few, yes, especially at first. But what surprised me is that the strongest resistance came from parents, not teachers. Some complained that their children were just playing at school. So now we run evening sessions where parents play the game themselves. After an hour of arguing about solar subsidies, they're usually completely won over."],["I","Is there any evidence that the games actually work?"],["G","A university in Cuenca did a small study with twelve classes. Students who'd played Voltage scored noticeably higher on an electricity test than those who'd had normal lessons. But I'm wary of overstating it. It was a small study, and the teachers who volunteered were probably the enthusiastic ones. What I'm more confident about is motivation: attendance goes up on game days, and quieter students participate far more."],["I","What's your next game?"],["G","I'd love to say it's a sequel to Voltage, but no. Lots of people have asked for a game about climate change, and maybe one day. Right now I'm working on a history game based on the Qhapaq Ñan, the Inca road system, where players are messengers carrying information between towns. It's for primary schools, so the challenge is keeping the rules simple enough."],["I","Finally, any advice for teachers who want to use games in class?"],["G","Start with a game you genuinely enjoy yourself. If you're bored, they'll be bored. And whatever you do, don't skip the conversation at the end. The real learning often happens when students talk about why they won or lost."]],
q:[["What made Martín decide to create his first game?","seeing how focused his nephews were while playing","a field trip to a hydroelectric plant","his students asking for more interactive lessons",0],["What does Martín say is the classic mistake in educational games?","They are too difficult for children.","The learning is added on rather than built in.","They depend too much on dice.",1],["What was the most difficult part of designing Voltage?","making it fair for all players","reducing the playing time","designing the artwork",0],["Who was most resistant to using the game in schools?","teachers","students","parents",2],["What is Martín most confident about regarding the effects of his games?","They improve test scores dramatically.","They make students more motivated.","They work equally well with all teachers.",1],["What is Martín's next game about?","climate change","the Inca road system","a new version of the electricity grid",1],["What does Martín recommend teachers do after playing a game?","discuss the results with the students","give the students a written test","choose a game the students enjoy",0]]},
{title:"Interview with a science journalist who covers space",intro:"You will hear an interview with Sophie Clarke, a science journalist who writes about space, asteroids and the search for life.",voices:{I:V_.ada,G:V_.lil},
script:[["I","My guest today is Sophie Clarke, a science journalist who has been covering space for almost twenty years. Sophie, welcome."],["G","Thanks for having me."],["I","You've written a lot about asteroids recently. Why do you think the public is suddenly so interested?"],["G","I suspect it's largely down to one particular object, known as 2024 YR4. When it was discovered, the estimated chance of it hitting Earth in 2032 climbed, for a few weeks, to about three per cent, which was the highest figure ever recorded for an object of that size. Headlines went wild. Then, as more observations came in, the risk dropped to practically zero. Apparently there's still a small chance it could hit the Moon, which is rather fascinating. But I think what really caught people's attention was realising that this is something astronomers genuinely keep an eye on."],["I","How did you feel, as a journalist, about the way that story was reported?"],["G","Mixed feelings, honestly. Some of the coverage was excellent. But a few outlets ran headlines about a city killer heading for Earth, which, technically, wasn't false, but it gave a completely distorted impression. What frustrated me most was that very few explained why the probability rises before it falls. When you first spot an asteroid, the range of possible paths is very wide, and Earth sits inside it. As you narrow it down, the probability often goes up briefly before Earth drops out of the range altogether. That's perfectly normal, but it was hardly ever explained."],["I","Could we actually stop an asteroid if one were heading our way?"],["G","Possibly, yes. In 2022, NASA's DART mission deliberately crashed a spacecraft into a small asteroid called Dimorphos, and it changed its orbit by around half an hour, which was far more than anyone had expected. That said, Dimorphos is tiny, and we had years of warning. I'd be wary of anyone who says we're now fully protected. The European mission Hera is meant to study the results in detail, and until that's done, I'd say we've proved the concept, nothing more."],["I","Your other big area is the search for life. Are we close?"],["G","I'm tempted to say closer than ever, but I've learnt to be careful. Every few months there's a new claim: a gas on a distant planet, an odd rock on Mars. Each one is genuinely interesting. But in my experience, the first announcement is rarely the last word. My rule as a journalist is to wait for an independent team to look at the data before I use the word evidence. It isn't always popular with my editors."],["I","What story are you most excited about at the moment?"],["G","Funnily enough, nothing very far away. In 2029, an asteroid called Apophis will pass closer to Earth than some of our satellites, and on a clear night it should be visible without a telescope from parts of Europe and Africa. When it was first spotted, it was thought there might be a chance of impact, but that's now been ruled out. I find the idea of millions of people simply looking up together quite moving. It's a chance to get people excited about science without any fear involved."],["I","Do you think the money spent on space is justified, given the problems we have here on Earth?"],["G","It's a fair question, and I don't dismiss it. But I'd point out that space research isn't a separate world. Weather satellites, GPS and much of what we know about climate change all came from it. The sample brought back from the asteroid Bennu contained amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, which may tell us something about how life on Earth began. That said, I don't think every mission is worth it. Space tourism for billionaires, for instance, I find very hard to justify."],["I","Finally, what advice would you give someone who wants to become a science journalist?"],["G","Learn to read a scientific paper, not just the press release. Press releases are written to get attention, and they sometimes overstate things. And don't be afraid to ask scientists the simple questions. If you don't understand something, your readers won't either."]],
q:[["Why does Sophie think public interest in asteroids has increased?","An asteroid is certain to hit the Moon.","One asteroid briefly seemed to pose a real risk to Earth.","Astronomers have started monitoring asteroids for the first time.",1],["What frustrated Sophie most about the media coverage?","Journalists published false information.","Few reports explained why the probability changed.","Too few outlets covered the story.",1],["What is Sophie's view of the DART mission?","It shows the idea can work, but more research is needed.","It proves that Earth is now fully protected.","It was less successful than scientists had expected.",0],["What rule does Sophie follow when reporting possible signs of life?","She waits for other scientists to check the data.","She only reports discoveries made on Mars.","She lets her editors decide which words to use.",0],["Why is Sophie excited about Apophis?","It may collide with a satellite.","Many people will be able to see it with the naked eye.","It could still hit Earth in 2029.",1],["What is Sophie's opinion about spending money on space?","Every space mission is worth the money.","Much of it brings benefits, although not all of it.","The money should be spent on problems on Earth instead.",1],["What does Sophie advise future science journalists to do?","read the original research, not only the press release","write mainly for specialist readers","avoid asking scientists basic questions",0]]}
];
